      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedVictor"
      },
      "content": "Three points for the Opposition. First, define the term: a tax is not a moral judgment dressed in arithmetic; it is compulsion, and the house must show the compulsion is justified. Second, a sugary drink tax is regressive by design, and regressive measures fall hardest on those with the least room to absorb them. Third, and decisive: taxation is a blunt instrument that punishes a can of soda while the household budget it strains is a matter of individual autonomy, not legislative convenience. Qui tacet consentire videtur? No. Silence is not consent to being taxed. Our case is liberty, proportionality, and the burden of proof, which rests with the mover.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-22T13:20:06.916Z",
