        "knowledge": {
          "query": "This house would tax sugary drinks Victor Hale: Three points for the Opposition. First, define the term: a tax is not a…",
          "trigger": "gated",
          "retrieved": [
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraTri08",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara2",
              "title": "ED triage guidelines",
              "locator": "p. 2",
              "text": "## 2.4 Caffeine withdrawal\n\nCaffeine withdrawal headache usually begins 12 to 24 hours after a sharp cut in intake and can last two to nine days. Gradual tapering, fluids and simple painkillers are usually enough; it rarely needs emergency care.\n\nAsk about recent changes in coffee, tea, energy drinks and cola. Patients often do not connect a new headache with cutting back a week earlier. A slower taper (reducing by about a quarter every few days) prevents most withdrawal headaches.",
              "score": 0.01,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraFat06",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara1",
              "title": "Meridian Shift Fatigue Review 2025",
              "locator": "p. 2",
              "text": "## 1.1 What this review does not measure\n\nThe review relies on what staff report. It does not measure fatigue objectively, and it cannot prove that long blocks cause errors. It shows associations that are consistent across sites, departments and months. Where we discuss costs, we use the Trust finance office's standard figures.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraFat14",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara1",
              "title": "Meridian Shift Fatigue Review 2025",
              "locator": "p. 7",
              "text": "## 6. The compressed-rota pilot\n\nTwo wards piloted a compressed rota: four longer days, then three days off. After twelve weeks, burnout scores on both wards fell by roughly a fifth, sleep quality improved, and patient throughput stayed within normal variation.\n\nThe pilot wards were Ward 7 (acute medicine) and the emergency department's observation unit. Staff chose to join. The rota guaranteed the three rest days: they could not be cancelled except in a declared major incident.\n\nWhat changed after twelve weeks:\n\n- Burnout scores fell by 19% on Ward 7 and 22% on the observation unit.\n- Average sleep before a shift rose by 40 minutes.\n- Short-notice cancellations of rest days fell to zero, by design.\n- Patient throughput, length of stay and readmissions stayed within normal variation for those wards.\n\nStaff on the pilot wards described the guaranteed rest days, not the longer shifts, as the change that mattered most.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraFat16",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara1",
              "title": "Meridian Shift Fatigue Review 2025",
              "locator": "p. 9",
              "text": "## 8. Retention\n\nVoluntary resignations among nursing staff reached 14% over the year. The Trust's finance office puts each replacement at roughly four months of salary once recruitment, induction and supervised practice are counted.\n\nThe rate was highest in the emergency department (19%) and lowest in day surgery (6%). Two thirds of leavers had been with the Trust for under five years.\n\nReplacing a nurse is not only a cost. New starters need supervision, which takes experienced staff away from patients during the first months. Wards with high turnover were also the wards with the most short-notice rest-day cancellations, a cycle the review describes as \"exhaustion feeding exhaustion\" .",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraFat12",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara1",
              "title": "Meridian Shift Fatigue Review 2025",
              "locator": "p. 5",
              "text": "## 4.1 The commute home\n\nTwelve percent of respondents said they had nodded off while driving home after a night shift at least once during the review period. Three reported minor collisions. The Trust's car park sleep pods, piloted at Meridian General since March, were used by fewer than one in twenty eligible staff, mostly because staff wanted to get home to children.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraFat02",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara1",
              "title": "Meridian Shift Fatigue Review 2025",
              "locator": "p. 1",
              "text": "## Summary\n\nFatigue is no longer an occasional complaint among the Trust's clinical staff. It is a measurable, rising condition with a direct line to patient safety, sickness absence and resignations. This review followed 1,140 clinical staff for ten months and found that the length of consecutive shift blocks, not total hours alone, is the strongest predictor of how tired staff say they are.\n\nThe headline findings:\n\n- Staff working more than three consecutive long shifts reported fatigue scores 38% higher than colleagues on shorter blocks.\n- High burnout in the emergency department rose from 29% of staff in January to 41% in October.\n- Staff who slept under six hours before a shift were twice as likely to report a near-miss medication error that week.\n- Two wards that piloted a compressed rota saw burnout fall by roughly a fifth in twelve weeks, with no loss of patient throughput.\n- Voluntary nursing resignations reached 14% over the year, and each replacement costs about four months of salary.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraFat07",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara1",
              "title": "Meridian Shift Fatigue Review 2025",
              "locator": "p. 3",
              "text": "## 2. Shift patterns and fatigue\n\nThe single clearest pattern in the data is the effect of long blocks.\n\nStaff working more than three consecutive long shifts reported fatigue scores 38% higher than colleagues on shorter blocks. The effect was strongest in emergency and acute medicine, where rest days were most often cancelled at short notice.\n\nA \"long shift\" in this review is any shift of eleven hours or more. Across the Trust, 46% of clinical shifts were long shifts. In the emergency department the figure was 78%.\n\nTotal weekly hours mattered less than the way those hours were arranged. Two nurses working the same 37.5 hours could report very different fatigue: the one who worked four long shifts in a row, then had three days off, reported less fatigue than the one whose long shifts were scattered across the week with single rest days in between. Single rest days, especially after a night shift, were rarely restful.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_amaraFat08",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara1",
              "title": "Meridian Shift Fatigue Review 2025",
              "locator": "p. 3",
              "text": "## 2.1 Short-notice changes\n\nRest days cancelled with less than 48 hours' notice were the second strongest predictor of fatigue. In emergency and acute medicine, one rest day in nine was cancelled at short notice, usually to cover sickness. In community and day-surgery settings the figure was one in forty.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            }
          ]
        },
