      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedVictor"
      },
      "content": "The house must decide one thing: whether the burden of proof has been met. It has not. My learned friend concedes the tax is regressive, then asks you to trust that the revenue answers the objection. That is a promise dressed as a policy, and promises are not proof. Honest pricing, she says. Who defines honest? The state sets the price, collects the price, and spends the price, and calls that autonomy. No new argument is needed: a burden unmet is a burden lost. The Opposition rests.",
      "status": "complete",
