        "knowledge": {
          "query": "This house would tax sugary drinks Amara Okafor: Victor says silence isn't consent, but nobody consents to paying for s…",
          "trigger": "gated",
          "retrieved": [
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_victorMoot02",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedVictor1",
              "title": "Moot court notes",
              "locator": "¶ 2",
              "text": "¶ 5. Watch for the \"speed, not direction\" turn. Once the opposition accepts the goal, the debate is about implementation, and the side with a timetable wins it.\n\n¶ 6. Closing options: res ipsa loquitur; \"the destination is agreed\". Don't sound triumphant. Judges reward conceding a fair point gracefully.\n\n¶ 7. Definitions first. \"Nationwide\" means the default standard week in law, not an identical schedule for every employer. \"Adopt\" means legislate a standard with a transition, not switch overnight. If the opposition reads \"nationwide\" as \"identical\", correct it in the first rebuttal and keep correcting it.\n\n¶ 8. Their best argument is cost in coverage sectors. Do not deny it. Reframe it: the cost of exhaustion is already being paid, in sickness absence, agency fees and resignations. Ask for their number for the status quo.\n\n¶ 9. Questions to put to an economist: What is the cost of doing nothing? Which of your figures come from volunteers? Would you accept a staged timetable with sector trials written into it?",
              "score": 0.125,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_victorMoot01",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedVictor1",
              "title": "Moot court notes",
              "locator": "¶ 1",
              "text": "Moot court notes: \"This house would adopt a nationwide four-day work week\" V. Hale, for the Meridian Bar Society exhibition debate. Private notes.\n\n¶ 1. Expect the opposition to say the evidence base is narrow. Concede scope early, then shift the burden: the status quo is also a policy and has to justify itself.\n\n¶ 2. Historical anchor: the Meridian Working Hours Act 1931 made the two-day weekend the default. It gave agriculture and shipping a ten-year transition and exempted continuous services.\n\n¶ 3. Continuous-coverage sectors already run on shift rules under Part IV of the Working Time Code: rest periods, a cap on consecutive shifts, and averaging over a reference period. A shorter standard week sits inside that structure.\n\n¶ 4. Phase-in precedent: sector-by-sector timetables, collective agreements first, the public sector as model employer. Courts have upheld staged duties where the end point is clear.",
              "score": 0.01,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_victorMoot03",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedVictor1",
              "title": "Moot court notes",
              "locator": "¶ 3",
              "text": "¶ 10. Questions to expect from a physician on my own side: patient safety on longer shifts. Answer: the standard week limits total hours; Part IV still caps consecutive shifts.\n\n¶ 11. Latin, sparingly. \"Res ipsa loquitur\" (the thing speaks for itself) for the close. \"Audi alteram partem\" (hear the other side) if accused of ignoring their evidence. Always translate.\n\n¶ 12. Structure for every speech: three points, numbered, each with one example. Finish on the burden of proof.",
              "score": 0.015,
              "cited": false
            }
          ]
